  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(A\u2228B)=\u00acA\u2227\u00acB.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(3\\,|\\,18\\text{\u00a0or\u00a0}11\\text{\u00a0is not prime}) & \u2261\u00ac((3\\,|\\,18)\u2228(11\\text{\u00a0is not prime})) \\\\  & \u2261\u00ac(3\\,|\\,18)\u2227\u00ac(11\\text{\u00a0is not prime}) \\\\  & \u2261(3\\,\\text{\u29f8}|\\,18)\u2227(11\\text{\u00a0is prime}) \\\\  & \u22613\\,\\text{\u29f8}|\\,18\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}11\\text{\u00a0is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
